PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,B,D

Several colleagues correctly note that the 18-year-old cohort shrank almost exactly on the 1990s trajectory (roughly 2 M down to ~1.1 M), so A cannot be salvaged as an explanation. B is also weak once we look at the data more closely: the gross enrollment ratio has risen, but the marginal gains since the mid-2000s have been modest and concentrated among a narrowing slice of high-school graduates; they have not been large enough on their own to produce the observed absolute increase in entrants. D likewise misreads institutional dynamics—junior colleges have contracted sharply and the newer professional/vocational universities plus senmon-gakkō remain largely parallel or terminal tracks rather than systematic feeders, so they do not mechanically inflate four-year head-counts.

By contrast, C captures the documented (if still modest) growth in adult and recurrent learners in undergraduate and professional programs, while E correctly identifies the cumulative effect of repeated deregulation rounds and continued private-university subsidies that kept marginal institutions viable and expanded total capacity. The inappropriate set is therefore A, B, D.}

