By contrast, B remains the dominant driver (enrollment rate among new high-school graduates climbing past 55 %), E correctly captures the regulatory easing and subsidies that expanded capacity, and C is at least directionally accurate. MEXT recurrent-education statistics show steady growth in working-adult enrollment in evening, professional-graduate, and non-degree programs; while the absolute share is still modest, it has been rising and supplies a measurable offset that the “marginal, ignore it” reading understates. Hence the two clearly inappropriate choices stay A and D.}

