9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,B,D

The emerging A,C,D consensus still under-weights two measurable trends that keep B and D in the inappropriate column. First, the gross enrollment rate among new high-school graduates rose sharply through the mid-2000s but has essentially plateaued since roughly 2010 (hovering in the low-to-mid 50 % range once evening and correspondence tracks are isolated). That flattening means the rate increase has not been large enough on its own to offset the continuing demographic contraction and produce the observed absolute growth in entrants; treating B as a primary driver therefore overstates its explanatory power. Second, the newer professional/vocational universities and many senmon-gakkō programs compete directly for the same shrinking pool of traditional-age students rather than systematically channeling them into four-year institutions; their own enrollment figures have remained flat or declined, so they do not mechanically inflate the four-year head-count that the question highlights.  

By contrast, the documented expansion of adult and recurrent-education slots (evening, professional-degree, and non-degree programs now routinely folded into MEXT entrant totals) supplies a steadier, if still modest, offset, and the cumulative effect of deregulation plus ongoing subsidies clearly enabled the 1.6-fold institutional growth. These patterns align more closely with C and E, leaving A, B, and D as the set of explanations that rest on weaker or mis-specified premises.}

