14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,B,D

I appreciate the detailed exchanges, but the A,B,D set still holds up more cleanly once we separate headline undergraduate entrant counts from the broader “university” statistics that MEXT sometimes aggregates. A is weak because the realized 18-year-old contraction tracked the 1990s central projections quite closely; any modest variance around migration or high-school completion rates is too small to explain sustained or rising entrant totals. B is likewise overstated: the enrollment-rate gains among traditional high-school graduates have been modest since the late 2000s, have largely plateaued, and do not by themselves offset the demographic squeeze in absolute numbers. D remains structurally mismatched—junior colleges and senmon-gakkō have contracted sharply and operate mainly as competitors or terminal tracks rather than systematic feeders to four-year programs.

By contrast, C captures a real if still-modest expansion in adult and recurrent learners that appears in the official entrant series (especially evening, correspondence, and professional-degree routes), while E aligns with the documented regulatory easing and subsidy continuity that preserved institutional capacity. If the group is weighing which explanations are factually or conceptually mis-specified for the observed resilience in entrant head-counts, A,B,D continues to be the more defensible inappropriate set.}

