2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,D

The 18-year-old cohort contraction followed the 1990s projections almost exactly (~2 M down to ~1.1 M), so any claim of a milder-than-expected drop is factually unsupported and cannot explain sustained entrant numbers; A therefore belongs in the inappropriate set. D is likewise structurally mismatched—junior colleges have contracted sharply, senmon-gakkō function mainly as terminal or parallel tracks, and the newer professional/vocational universities remain too few and non-systematic to act as feeders inflating four-year head-counts.  

By contrast, B correctly identifies the dominant offset: the university advancement rate among new high-school graduates has risen from the low-30 % range to well above 50 %, more than compensating for the smaller cohort. E is equally well-documented; successive rounds of deregulation on establishment standards plus continued public subsidies enabled the 1.6-fold growth in institutions while keeping most of them viable. On C, while raw adult undergraduate head-counts remain modest, MEXT recurrent-education statistics show steady growth in evening, correspondence, and professional-degree programs now routinely folded into entrant totals, and the policy emphasis on reskilling has visibly expanded non-traditional entry routes. Labeling C inappropriate therefore overstates its marginality relative to the headline trend. This leaves A and D as the two clearly mismatched explanations.}

